    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with a very breathy, weak voice and difficulty swallowing liquids (occasional aspiration) 2 weeks after a total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows the left vocal fold is completely immobile and fixed in a paramedian position. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Unilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) paralysis", "Unilateral Superior Laryngeal Nerve (SLN) paralysis", "Spasmodic dysphonia", "Vocal fold nodule"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Unilateral RLN injury (common during thyroidectomy) paralyzes the ipsilateral intrinsic laryngeal muscles, leaving the cord in a paramedian position. The resulting glottic gap causes a breathy voice and aspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why does a patient with a unilateral RLN paralysis present with a breathy voice but NO respiratory distress?', '["The airway is completely blocked", "The paralyzed fold in the paramedian position leaves enough airway to breathe comfortably, but prevents complete glottic closure during phonation (glottic incompetence)", "The nerve will recover immediately", "The false vocal cords compensate for breathing", "The trachea expands"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The paramedian position provides an adequate airway for normal breathing. The breathiness is strictly due to air escaping through the unclosed glottic gap during speech.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a permanent unilateral vocal fold paralysis desires surgical intervention to improve her breathy voice and stop her aspiration. What is the standard surgical concept used to treat this?', '["Tracheostomy", "Vocal fold lateralization", "Vocal fold medialization (e.g., injection laryngoplasty or Type I thyroplasty) to push the paralyzed fold to the midline", "Total laryngectomy", "Arytenoidectomy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Medialization techniques push the paralyzed, lateralized vocal fold into the midline so the functioning contralateral vocal fold can meet it, closing the gap and restoring voice and swallowing safety.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which procedure involves placing a solid implant (e.g., Silastic or Gore-Tex) through a window cut in the thyroid cartilage to permanently push a paralyzed vocal fold to the midline?', '["Type I Thyroplasty (Medialization Laryngoplasty)", "Injection Laryngoplasty", "Arytenoid Adduction", "Cordotomy", "Laser ablation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Type I Thyroplasty is the gold standard for permanent medialization of a unilaterally paralyzed vocal fold.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female develops severe biphasic stridor and respiratory distress in the recovery room immediately following a difficult total thyroidectomy. Her voice, however, is surprisingly adequate. What is the most immediate life-threatening diagnosis?', '["Unilateral RLN paralysis", "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) paralysis", "Asthma attack", "Laryngeal papillomatosis", "Vocal fold hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral RLN paralysis leaves both vocal folds resting near the midline. This critically narrows the airway, causing severe stridor and distress. Paradoxically, the voice is often preserved because the cords are close enough to vibrate together.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most immediate, life-saving intervention for a patient with acute bilateral RLN paralysis who is in severe respiratory distress and failing to oxygenate?', '["Vocal therapy", "Immediate re-intubation or tracheostomy to secure the airway", "Steroid injections", "Botox injections", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The airway is mechanically obstructed at the glottis. It must be secured immediately to prevent death from asphyxiation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient with permanent bilateral vocal fold paralysis wishes to be decannulated (have their tracheostomy tube removed), what surgical trade-off is typically required?', '["There is no trade-off", "A procedure to permanently enlarge the airway (e.g., laser cordotomy or arytenoidectomy) will be performed, which will permanently worsen their voice, making it breathy", "Total laryngectomy", "A lung transplant", "Placement of a pacemaker"]'::jsonb, 1, 'To create a safe airway, the surgeon must remove a portion of the vocal fold or arytenoid. This opens the airway but permanently destroys glottic closure, destroying the voice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old professional soprano complains of vocal fatigue and the inability to sing high pitches following surgery for a parapharyngeal space tumor high in the neck. She does not have a breathy voice at normal speaking pitches. What nerve was likely injured?', '["Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "External branch of the Superior Laryngeal Nerve (SLN)", "Internal branch of the Superior Laryngeal Nerve (SLN)", "Hypoglossal nerve", "Glossopharyngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The external branch of the SLN innervates only the cricothyroid muscle, which elongates and tenses the vocal fold to elevate pitch. Injury causes loss of upper range and vocal fatigue without severe breathiness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During laryngoscopy, what subtle finding might suggest a Superior Laryngeal Nerve (SLN) paralysis?', '["The vocal folds are fixed near the midline", "Massive swelling of the vocal folds", "The vocal fold on the affected side appears slightly shortened and bowed, and the epiglottis or posterior commissure may deviate toward the paralyzed side during high-pitched phonation", "A large polyp is visible", "An anterior web is visible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the cricothyroid muscle cannot tense the cord, it appears shorter and bowed compared to the healthy side during phonation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with a voice that sounds severely "strained," "strangled," and constantly breaks, requiring immense effort to speak. Strikingly, her voice completely normalizes when she laughs, sings, or whispers. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Muscle Tension Dysphonia", "Adductor Spasmodic Dysphonia", "Vocal fold paralysis", "Laryngitis", "Parkinson''s disease"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Adductor spasmodic dysphonia is a focal dystonia causing the thyroarytenoid muscles to forcefully spasm shut during speech. It is "task-specific," so non-speech tasks (laughing, singing) are unaffected.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Is Spasmodic Dysphonia primarily a structural (anatomic) or a functional/neurological disorder?', '["Structural; the vocal folds are damaged", "Functional/Neurological; the vocal folds themselves are perfectly healthy and structurally normal, but the neurological signal driving them is abnormal", "Infectious; caused by a virus", "Autoimmune; caused by antibodies", "Neoplastic; caused by a tumor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Spasmodic dysphonia is a central nervous system disorder (a focal dystonia), not a problem with the laryngeal anatomy itself.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the gold-standard symptomatic treatment for Adductor Spasmodic Dysphonia?', '["Psychotherapy", "Systemic muscle relaxants", "Botulinum toxin (Botox) injections into the bilateral thyroarytenoid muscles", "Total laryngectomy", "Radiation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Botox temporarily weakens the spastic thyroarytenoid muscles, preventing the severe hyperadductive spasms and providing significant relief for 3-4 months per injection.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does Abductor Spasmodic Dysphonia differ clinically from the Adductor subtype?', '["It is caused by vocal abuse", "Instead of clamping shut, the vocal folds spasm OPEN during speech (especially during voiceless consonants), causing sudden breathy, whispering voice breaks", "It causes airway obstruction", "It is treated with antibiotics", "It causes a deep, gravelly voice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Abductor SD affects the posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscles, causing them to inappropriately abduct the cords during speech.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If Botox is used to treat Abductor Spasmodic Dysphonia, which specific muscle must be targeted for injection?', '["Thyroarytenoid (TA) muscle", "Cricothyroid muscle", "Posterior Cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle", "Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle", "Interarytenoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because the PCA is the sole abductor, it is the muscle spasming in Abductor SD. Injecting it is technically more challenging and carries a small risk of airway compromise if both PCAs are overly weakened.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 80-year-old male complains his voice has become weak, thin, and breathy over the last few years. He has no history of smoking or surgery. Laryngoscopy reveals bilaterally thin, bowed vocal folds with a prominent spindle-shaped gap between them during phonation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Vocal fold nodules", "Laryngeal cancer", "Presbyphonia (Vocal fold atrophy)", "Spasmodic dysphonia", "Laryngitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Presbyphonia is age-related atrophy of the thyroarytenoid muscle and the superficial lamina propria. The loss of bulk causes the vocal folds to bow, creating a gap that leaks air.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a common surgical intervention to improve the voice in a patient with severe Presbyphonia if voice therapy fails?', '["Tracheostomy", "Vocal cord stripping", "Vocal fold augmentation (injection laryngoplasty) to ''plump up'' the atrophic folds with a filler (e.g., hyaluronic acid, fat, or calcium hydroxylapatite) and improve closure", "Total laryngectomy", "Botox injection"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Adding bulk to the atrophic vocal folds allows them to meet in the midline again, restoring glottic competence and a strong voice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old female elite gymnast presents with episodes of sudden, severe shortness of breath and loud inspiratory stridor that occur strictly during high-intensity workouts. She feels a "choking" sensation. Albuterol provides zero relief. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Exercise-induced asthma", "Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion (PVFM / Vocal Cord Dysfunction)", "Bilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Laryngeal cancer", "Croup"]'::jsonb, 1, 'PVFM involves the involuntary closure of the vocal folds during inspiration (when they should be open). It mimics asthma but causes inspiratory stridor in the neck, not expiratory wheezing in the chest, and does not respond to bronchodilators.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the hallmark physiological finding seen on flexible laryngoscopy during an attack of Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion (PVFM)?', '["The vocal cords are completely paralyzed and flaccid", "Involuntary, paradoxical ADDUCTION (closure) of the true vocal folds during inspiration", "Massive swelling of the epiglottis", "A large polyp blocking the airway", "The vocal cords remain completely abducted during phonation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocal folds inappropriately clamp shut when the patient tries to breathe in, causing functional airway obstruction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary, first-line treatment for Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion (PVFM)?', '["High-dose inhaled corticosteroids", "Emergency tracheostomy", "Speech therapy (respiratory retraining, laryngeal relaxation exercises) and psychological support/biofeedback", "Botox injections", "Surgical removal of the vocal folds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Patients are taught specific breathing techniques (like "sniffing" or breathing through pursed lips) to break the spasm and "release" the vocal folds during an attack. Addressing underlying anxiety is also crucial.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with Parkinson''s disease commonly exhibits which type of voice change?', '["A loud, booming voice", "A high-pitched, squeaky voice", "A hypophonic (very soft, weak), monotone, and breathy voice", "A severely strained, strangled voice", "A voice with frequent pitch breaks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rigidity and bradykinesia of Parkinson''s affect the respiratory and laryngeal muscles, resulting in reduced vocal volume (hypophonia) and a monotone pitch.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What specific speech therapy program is highly effective and widely used for improving the voice in patients with Parkinson''s disease?', '["Resonant voice therapy", "Lee Silverman Voice Treatment (LSVT LOUD)", "Whisper therapy", "Singing therapy", "Biofeedback"]'::jsonb, 1, 'LSVT LOUD is a specialized, intensive behavioral treatment program designed specifically to increase vocal loudness and improve articulation in Parkinson''s patients by "recalibrating" their perception of normal loudness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Essential Vocal Tremor primarily affects which aspect of the voice?', '["It causes complete voice loss", "It causes rhythmic, regular fluctuations in pitch and loudness during sustained phonation (e.g., holding the vowel ''ah'')", "It causes a breathy whisper", "It causes sudden voice arrests only on voiceless consonants", "It causes vocal fatigue without any pitch change"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Essential tremor is a neurologic disorder causing a 4-7 Hz rhythmic tremor of the laryngeal muscles, which is easily heard as a "shaky" voice when the patient sustains a vowel.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How can you clinically differentiate Essential Vocal Tremor from Adductor Spasmodic Dysphonia?', '["Essential tremor only occurs when whispering", "Essential tremor is rhythmic and present on sustained vowels, while Spasmodic Dysphonia causes irregular spasms that are worse during connected speech and absent during singing/laughing", "Essential tremor only affects children", "Spasmodic Dysphonia causes a breathy voice", "There is no difference"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Spasmodic dysphonia is task-specific and irregular; essential tremor is rhythmic and present across most vocal tasks, including sustaining a vowel.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient has a combined presentation of Spasmodic Dysphonia and Essential Tremor, what is the most appropriate medical treatment?', '["Total laryngectomy", "Botulinum toxin (Botox) injections into the thyroarytenoid muscles (often improves both the spasms and the tremor)", "Antibiotics", "Steroids", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Botox is the mainstay of treatment for SD and can also significantly dampen the amplitude of an essential vocal tremor.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the term for a voice disorder caused by psychological trauma or severe stress, where the patient has structurally normal vocal folds but can only speak in a whisper (aphonia)?', '["Presbyphonia", "Spasmodic Dysphonia", "Functional (Psychogenic) Aphonia", "Vocal fold paralysis", "Reinke''s edema"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Functional aphonia is a conversion disorder. The vocal folds are structurally normal and will close normally during a cough or throat clear, proving the neurologic and anatomic pathways are intact.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a key diagnostic maneuver to confirm Functional (Psychogenic) Aphonia during a laryngoscopic exam?', '["Ask the patient to sing", "Ask the patient to cough or clear their throat; a crisp, loud cough confirms the vocal folds CAN fully adduct, ruling out paralysis or structural deficits", "Ask the patient to whisper", "Ask the patient to swallow", "Perform a biopsy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Coughing is a reflexive action. If the vocal folds slam shut forcefully during a cough, they are not paralyzed, confirming the aphonia is functional.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which neurologic disease, characterized by upper and lower motor neuron degeneration, frequently presents with a mixed spastic-flaccid dysarthria, hypernasality, and a strained, "wet" sounding voice?', '["Multiple Sclerosis", "Parkinson''s Disease", "Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS / Lou Gehrig''s disease)", "Myasthenia Gravis", "Essential Tremor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ALS profoundly affects bulbar function. The loss of motor control leads to a strained voice, poor articulation, and pooling of secretions (causing a "wet" sound).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old female presents with a voice that starts strong in the morning but becomes progressively weaker, breathy, and severely fatigued by the evening. She also notes occasional double vision at the end of the day. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Spasmodic Dysphonia", "Myasthenia Gravis", "Vocal fold nodules", "LPR", "Essential Tremor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Myasthenia Gravis involves autoantibodies against acetylcholine receptors. It classically causes fatiguing muscle weakness that worsens with use and improves with rest.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What diagnostic test is used to confirm the laryngeal involvement of Myasthenia Gravis?', '["CT scan of the neck", "Tensilon (edrophonium) test or checking for ACh receptor antibodies", "Laryngeal biopsy", "Barium swallow", "PET scan"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Tensilon test (or Ice Pack test for ptosis) rapidly restores muscle strength by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, confirming the diagnosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a breathy voice. Laryngoscopy shows the left vocal fold is immobile in the lateral (abducted) position. The patient also has a weak left shoulder shrug and deviation of the tongue to the left. What is the most likely anatomic location of the lesion?', '["The left recurrent laryngeal nerve in the chest", "The jugular foramen (causing Vernet syndrome or similar skull base syndrome affecting CN IX, X, XI, and potentially XII)", "The right cerebral hemisphere", "The superior laryngeal nerve", "The cricoarytenoid joint"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiple cranial nerve deficits (Vagus, Spinal Accessory, Hypoglossal) indicate a high lesion at the skull base (e.g., glomus jugulare tumor) before the nerves exit the cranium.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a high vagal lesion (at the skull base) will have paralysis of both the recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves. What position will the vocal fold typically assume?', '["Midline", "Paramedian", "Intermediate (cadaveric) position, which is more lateral than paramedian, causing a severely breathy voice and high risk of aspiration", "Fully abducted", "Fully adducted"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Loss of the RLN brings the cord toward the midline, but the SLN (cricothyroid) normally provides a slight adductory pull. If BOTH are lost, the cord falls into the wider "cadaveric" position.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary role of Laryngeal Electromyography (LEMG) in the workup of vocal fold paralysis?', '["To cure the paralysis", "To distinguish between true nerve injury (neuropathy) and mechanical fixation of the cricoarytenoid joint, and to provide prognostic information regarding nerve recovery", "To measure the size of the airway", "To detect cancer", "To treat Spasmodic Dysphonia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'LEMG measures the electrical activity of the laryngeal muscles. Normal activity in an immobile cord indicates a dislocated or arthritic joint. Fibrillation potentials indicate denervation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a unilateral vocal fold paralysis asks how long they should wait for spontaneous recovery before considering a permanent surgical intervention (like a Type I Thyroplasty). What is the standard recommendation?', '["1 week", "1 month", "6 to 12 months, as the recurrent laryngeal nerve can regenerate or the contralateral cord can compensate over time", "5 years", "Never"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unless the nerve is known to be severed, most surgeons wait at least 6-12 months for spontaneous recovery. Temporary injections (e.g., hyaluronic acid) can be used during this waiting period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During an injection laryngoplasty for vocal fold paralysis, where exactly is the filler material injected?', '["Into the superficial lamina propria", "Into the epithelium", "Deep into the thyroarytenoid (vocalis) muscle or paraglottic space to push the medial edge of the vocal fold toward the midline", "Into the cricoid cartilage", "Into the trachea"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The goal is to add bulk deep within the fold to push the vibrating edge medially, without stiffening the delicate superficial lamina propria (Reinke''s space).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following materials is commonly used for a TEMPORARY injection laryngoplasty (lasting 2-6 months)?', '["Gore-Tex", "Silastic block", "Hyaluronic acid or Carboxymethylcellulose (Radiesse Voice Gel)", "Calcium hydroxylapatite", "Autologous fat"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hyaluronic acid and CMC are temporary, resorbable fillers used while waiting to see if a paralyzed nerve will recover.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a PERMANENT or long-lasting injectable material for vocal fold augmentation?', '["Hyaluronic acid", "Saline", "Calcium hydroxylapatite (Radiesse Voice) or Autologous Fat", "Botulinum toxin", "Steroids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Calcium hydroxylapatite and fat are used when the paralysis is deemed permanent and the patient prefers injection over open framework surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a left vocal fold paralysis undergoes a Type I Thyroplasty. During the procedure, the surgeon asks the patient to speak. Why is this procedure typically performed under local anesthesia with light sedation?', '["To save money", "Because general anesthesia is dangerous", "To allow real-time voice feedback from the awake patient, so the surgeon can adjust the size and position of the implant to achieve the best possible voice", "To prevent bleeding", "To monitor brain waves"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Real-time auditory feedback is crucial. The surgeon custom-carves the implant or adds Gore-Tex until the patient''s voice sounds optimal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is an Arytenoid Adduction procedure?', '["Removing the arytenoid cartilage", "Injecting the arytenoid with Botox", "A surgical procedure often combined with a Thyroplasty that pulls the muscular process of the arytenoid anteriorly, rotating the vocal process medially to close a large posterior glottic gap", "A procedure to treat LPR", "A treatment for Spasmodic Dysphonia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A standard Type I thyroplasty pushes the middle of the cord over, but may not close a large gap at the posterior commissure. Arytenoid adduction specifically corrects this posterior gap.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe multiple sclerosis presents with a weak, breathy voice and frequent coughing while drinking. What is the most critical evaluation required before any surgical voice intervention?', '["A hearing test", "A formal swallowing evaluation (e.g., Modified Barium Swallow or FEES) to assess the risk of silent aspiration", "A PET scan", "A biopsy", "A sleep study"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In neurologic diseases, dysphagia and silent aspiration are major risks. Improving glottic closure often helps swallowing, but evaluating the full swallow mechanism is mandatory.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male presents with hoarseness. Laryngoscopy reveals a paralyzed right vocal fold. He also complains of a 20-pound weight loss and a chronic cough. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Vocal fold nodule", "Reinke''s edema", "Apical lung tumor (Pancoast tumor) compressing the right recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Spasmodic dysphonia", "Laryngitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The right RLN loops under the right subclavian artery in the lung apex. An apical lung cancer can compress it, causing paralysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a known thoracic aortic aneurysm develops sudden hoarseness. Which nerve has been compressed?', '["Right recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Left recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Right superior laryngeal nerve", "Left superior laryngeal nerve", "Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The left RLN loops under the aortic arch. An expanding aneurysm in this location (or an enlarged left atrium in mitral stenosis, known as Ortner''s syndrome) can stretch and paralyze the nerve.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is Ortner''s Syndrome (Cardiovocal Syndrome)?', '["Hoarseness caused by vocal nodules", "Hoarseness caused by left recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis secondary to cardiovascular pathology (e.g., massive left atrial enlargement from mitral stenosis compressing the nerve against the pulmonary artery)", "Hoarseness caused by a heart attack", "Hoarseness caused by high blood pressure", "Hoarseness caused by acid reflux"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It highlights the complex intrathoracic course of the left RLN.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a deep, rough, "growling" voice. Laryngoscopy shows the true vocal folds are immobile, and the patient is squeezing their false vocal folds tightly together to produce sound. What is this compensatory mechanism called?', '["Spasmodic Dysphonia", "Ventricular phonation (false vocal fold phonation)", "Presbyphonia", "Paradoxical Vocal Fold Motion", "Vocal tremor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When the true cords fail (due to paralysis or severe scarring), patients often unconsciously recruit the false cords to create sound. The resulting voice is characteristically rough and strained.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary treatment goal for a patient utilizing Ventricular Phonation?', '["Surgically remove the false vocal folds", "Voice therapy to ''unload'' the false folds and encourage true vocal fold vibration, often combined with medialization if the true cords are paralyzed", "Botox the false vocal folds", "Radiation", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Treatment focuses on correcting the underlying true cord deficit (e.g., medialization) so the patient no longer needs to compensate with the false cords.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a potential side effect of Botox injection for Adductor Spasmodic Dysphonia that the patient must be warned about?', '["Permanent loss of voice", "A temporary period (1-2 weeks) of a weak, breathy voice and mild difficulty swallowing liquids (choking) immediately after the injection before the voice stabilizes", "Severe airway obstruction", "Development of laryngeal cancer", "Growth of vocal nodules"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Botox initially over-weakens the thyroarytenoid muscles, causing glottic incompetence (breathiness/mild aspiration) until the effect slightly wears off into the "sweet spot" of fluent speech.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a severe traumatic brain injury is extubated but found to have absent protective laryngeal reflexes, leading to massive, life-threatening aspiration of saliva and food. If recovery is not expected, what surgical procedure completely separates the airway from the digestive tract?', '["Tracheostomy alone", "Total laryngectomy or Laryngotracheal separation", "Type I Thyroplasty", "Cordotomy", "Epiglottopexy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A tracheostomy alone does NOT prevent aspiration (secretions pool above the cuff and leak down). To definitively stop aspiration in a non-functioning larynx, the airway must be surgically separated from the esophagus.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "gag reflex" evaluating, and which cranial nerves are involved?', '["Sensory: CN V, Motor: CN VII", "Sensory: CN IX (Glossopharyngeal), Motor: CN X (Vagus)", "Sensory: CN X, Motor: CN XII", "Sensory: CN VII, Motor: CN IX", "Sensory: CN I, Motor: CN II"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The afferent limb is the glossopharyngeal nerve (sensing the touch on the posterior pharynx), and the efferent limb is the vagus nerve (causing the pharyngeal muscles to contract).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a left vagal nerve injury above the nodose ganglion will present with which combination of deficits?', '["Left vocal fold paralysis only", "Left vocal fold paralysis AND loss of sensation to the left supraglottis (due to SLN involvement)", "Bilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Right vocal fold paralysis", "No laryngeal deficits"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A high vagal lesion affects both the SLN (branching high) and the RLN (branching low), resulting in a paralyzed vocal fold in the cadaveric position and an anesthetic, high-risk aspirating larynx on that side.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common cause of unilateral vocal fold paralysis?', '["Idiopathic (often presumed viral neuropathy)", "Iatrogenic surgical injury (especially thyroid/parathyroid, anterior cervical spine, or thoracic surgeries)", "Malignancy (lung, thyroid, skull base)", "Trauma", "Neurologic disease (ALS, MS)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Surgical injury is the leading identifiable cause. However, a significant percentage (up to 30%) remain "idiopathic" even after exhaustive workup, likely due to a post-viral neuropathy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a sudden onset of a severely breathy voice. They report having a severe upper respiratory infection one week ago. Exam shows a unilateral vocal fold paralysis. Workup (including CT neck/chest) is completely negative. What is the diagnosis and prognosis?', '["Cancer; poor prognosis", "Idiopathic (post-viral) vocal fold paralysis; fair to good prognosis, as many will spontaneously recover function within 6-12 months", "Spasmodic dysphonia; requires Botox", "Myasthenia gravis; requires steroids", "LPR; requires PPIs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Post-viral neuropathy is a common cause of sudden vocal fold paralysis. Patients are typically observed (or offered temporary injection laryngoplasty) because spontaneous recovery rates are relatively high.')
